synapse-trainer examples
========================

recipe.json
-----------
A complete training-recipe.json. Fields other than `datasets` all have
defaults (see synapse_trainer/recipe.py DEFAULTS); `datasets[].weight` must
sum to ~1.0 and `split` must sum to ~1.0.

  python -m synapse_trainer inspect-arch --recipe recipe.json
  python -m synapse_trainer train --recipe recipe.json --data ./data --out ./bundle

`inspect-arch` needs only numpy. `train` needs PyTorch.


recipe.multi-dataset.json  +  data/
-----------------------------------
The PROJECT.md §14 mixture — 70% Copenhagen baseline, 20% lab attack corpus,
10% reviewed local detections — against the `data/` fixture tree below.

  python -m synapse_trainer inspect-recipe \
      --recipe recipe.multi-dataset.json --data data
  python -m synapse_trainer train \
      --recipe recipe.multi-dataset.json --data data --out ./bundle --dry-run

Both are torch-free: they resolve every dataset, split each one, apply the
weights and print the plan (per-dataset rows, effective weights, split sizes,
label distribution, imbalance warnings). Drop `--dry-run` to actually train.

`data/` deliberately exercises all three layouts the resolver understands:

  data/
    hq-copenhagen/
      baseline-2026-08/            <- versioned: v10 wins over v9 (numeric sort)
        v9/{dataset.csv,manifest.json}
        v10/{dataset.csv,manifest.json}
      reviewed-anomalies-2026-09/  <- canonical: <id>/dataset.csv
        {dataset.csv,manifest.json}
    thugs/
      lab-attacks-2026-08.csv      <- flat: <id>.csv
      lab-attacks-2026-08.manifest.json

Resolution order for a recipe entry {"id": "<id>"} under `--data ROOT`:

  1. the entry's explicit "path" (absolute, else relative to ROOT, else to cwd)
  2. ROOT/<id>.csv
  3. ROOT/<id>/dataset.csv
  4. ROOT/<id>/<latest version dir>/dataset.csv   (numeric-aware: v10 > v9)
  5. any single ROOT/<id>/*.csv

Nothing found is an error listing every path tried. A manifest.json (or
dataset.json) next to the CSV — or ROOT/<id>.manifest.json for the flat layout —
supplies the §14 dataset metadata; its `content_hash` is recorded in the bundle's
training-recipe.json so a model is traceable to the exact data version. The
manifest never overrides the CSV: it is metadata, not data.

These fixtures are synthetic and tiny — enough to demonstrate resolution,
weighting and the split, far too small to train anything useful.


Dataset CSV
-----------
`--data` is the dataset root the recipe's ids resolve under; a single CSV file
(or a directory containing `dataset.csv`, or the first *.csv found) still works
when the recipe names exactly one dataset. Each header must contain the 48
flow-features-v1 columns
(named by their frozen schema name; order in the file does not matter) plus a
`label` column. Extra columns are ignored; a missing feature column is an error.

Header (48 features, in frozen index order, + label):

flow_duration,packets_forward,packets_backward,bytes_forward,bytes_backward,packet_size_mean,packet_size_min,packet_size_max,packet_size_stddev,forward_packet_size_mean,backward_packet_size_mean,packets_per_second,bytes_per_second,forward_packets_per_second,backward_packets_per_second,interarrival_mean,interarrival_min,interarrival_max,interarrival_stddev,forward_interarrival_mean,backward_interarrival_mean,source_port,destination_port,protocol_tcp,protocol_udp,protocol_icmp,tcp_syn_count,tcp_ack_count,tcp_fin_count,tcp_rst_count,tcp_psh_count,tcp_urg_count,syn_ack_ratio,packet_direction_ratio,byte_direction_ratio,initial_tcp_window,average_tcp_window,down_up_bytes_ratio,small_packet_ratio,large_packet_ratio,average_payload_length,bidirectional_flag,internal_to_internal,internal_to_external,external_to_internal,external_to_external,dest_port_is_wellknown,snapshot_index,label

Notes:
  - `label` is a traffic-classes-v1 class NAME (normal, scan, dos_ddos,
    brute_force, botnet_c2, web_attack, suspicious) or its integer id 0..6.
  - Feature values are the RAW flow-features-v1 numbers. Do NOT pre-normalize:
    the trainer fits a normalizer on the training split and writes it to
    normalizer.json so the Go daemon can reproduce the transform.
  - Each row is one flow record: a closed flow, or a periodic snapshot of a
    long-lived flow (feature 47, `snapshot_index`, > 0).
  - An optional `id` column names the row. It is never a feature; it is used
    for the recorded per-split row identities (`<dataset id>#<row id>`) that
    make the "no test row ever reaches training" guarantee checkable. Without
    it the loader falls back to `<file stem>:<line number>`.

`dataset.sample.csv` in this directory is a tiny synthetic example with the
correct header. It is illustrative only — far too small to train anything.
